 30, p4 for which cos y = x. The “Arc” in Arcsine and Arccosine For a unit circle and radian angles, the arc length equation s = ru becomes"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.59389,"w":0.59307,"h":0.03002,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The graph of y = sin-1x (Figure 1.65b) is symmetric about the origin (it lies along the graph of x = sin y). The arcsine is therefore an odd function:"},{"x":0.09756,"y":0.60262,"w":0.22998,"h":0.09652,"fs":0.01151,"n":7,"k":1,"t":"s = u, so central angles and the arcs they subtend have the same measure. If x = sin y, then, in addition to being the angle whose sine is x, y is also the length of arc on the unit circle that subtends an angle whose sine is x. So we call y “the arc whose sine is x.”"},{"x":0.58631,"y":0.63226,"w":0.36521,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"sin-1(-x) = -sin-1x. (2)"},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.65528,"w":0.41723,"h":0.01467,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The graph of y = cos-1x (Figure 1.67b) has no such symmetry."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.68739,"w":0.29178,"h":0.02771,"fs":0.01407,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"EXAMPLE 8 Evaluate (a) sin-1 a 23"},{"x":0.63762,"y":0.68739,"w":0.18299,"h":0.03491,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"2 b and (b) cos-1 a- 1"},{"x":0.81243,"y":0.69187,"w":0.02449,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"2b ."},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.73534,"w":0.10445,"h":0.02899,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Solution (a) We see that"},{"x":0.12159,"y":0.73618,"w":0.06417,"h":0.01461,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"x2 + y2 = 1"},{"x":0.21775,"y":0.73991,"w":0.10177,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Arc whose sine is x"},{"x":0.59836,"y":0.76643,"w":0.06879,"h":0.02771,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"sin-1 a 23"},{"x":0.6504,"y":0.7709,"w":0.06532,"h":0.03044,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"2 b = p 3"},{"x":0.29349,"y":0.77609,"w":0.05633,"h":0.02251,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Arc whose cosine is x"},{"x":0.13187,"y":0.78806,"w":0.0687,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Angle whose"},{"x":0.15839,"y":0.80032,"w":0.04217,"h":0.0102